Conformance
Id	Clause
conformance.1	This module specification describes the module that reads Claude Code's recording of the raw API bodies [CLAUDE-CODE] as the input values of the living-memory specification, each kept unit once. It is a module specification of the living-memory specification, and restates nothing that specification states; the schema of the kept units is that of the messages module.
conformance.2	The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "REQUIRED", "SHALL", "SHALL NOT", "SHOULD", "SHOULD NOT", "RECOMMENDED", "NOT RECOMMENDED", "MAY", and "OPTIONAL" in this specification are to be interpreted as described in BCP 14 [BCP14] when, and only when, they appear in all capitals, as shown here.
conformance.3	The Rules are normative. Each rule states one requirement, and its Id names what it governs and the requirement's number: recording, what the module reads and what of it is spent; values, the input values it gives; map, what it gives of the requests' tree. This specification has no optional features.
Rules
Id	Rule
recording.1	With OTEL_LOG_RAW_API_BODIES set to file:<dir>, each successful response appends one line to <dir>/index.jsonl: timestamp, session_id, query_source, model, request_id, message_id, message_uuid, request_file and response_file; the request body is the JSON-serialized Messages API request parameters, and the response body the JSON-serialized Messages API response [CLAUDE-CODE]. The module MUST read a folder that holds index.jsonl, each of its lines a JSON value, the first value 1 [JSONL]; the session transcript is not read, its format being internal to Claude Code [CLAUDE-CODE].
recording.2	Claude's extended thinking is always redacted from the bodies [CLAUDE-CODE], and the module MUST give a thinking block as the bodies hold it.
recording.3	A request_file or response_file that is not an absolute path MUST be read in the recording's folder.
recording.4	An index line may be written before the files it names, and the module MUST stop at a line whose files are not yet written, and read it at the next conversion.
recording.5	An index line may name no request file, and such a line MUST give no value, its response being re-sent in the next request [CLAUDE-CODE].
recording.6	With file:<dir>, the untruncated bodies are written to request and response files under the directory, which is shipped with a log collector or sidecar [CLAUDE-CODE], and no source removes them. The module MUST name as spent the request and response files of every index line of an input up to the last one its output holds, but the files of the latest request of each of the input's threads that the output holds, which the next conversion reads again (values.6); index.jsonl is never spent.
values.1	A unit is a system block, or the system prompt where it is a string, a tool, a message, or a response's content, which is the message that follows [ANTHROPIC-API]. The request is the observation, and the module MUST give one value for each index line that names a request, in the index's order.
values.2	Each request carries the entire conversation history [CLAUDE-CODE], so it repeats an earlier request's messages; its parent is, of the latest request of each thread of its session, one query_source, the one whose messages, then response, its own messages agree with furthest, the later where two agree as far. Each value MUST name as extends its parent's index line, 0 where none agrees with its first message, and as kept how many of the parent's messages it keeps.
values.3	Two instances are equal when of the same type and value [JSON-SCHEMA-07], each unit compared whole as messages.mtsv keeps it (kept.2), the parent's response as the message of its role, assistant, and its content, which is all it holds. Each value MUST hold only the units its parent does not: its messages after those it keeps, each system block and tool unequal to the parent's at its pointer or beyond the parent's, a system prompt that is a string, and its response's content; each unit at its JSON Pointer in the request [RFC6901] as request.pointer, under the member that holds it, system, tools, messages or content.
values.4	Each value MUST name its request by index_line, its index line's number counted from 1 [JSONL], a name the Fields of the living-memory specification do not give, and by session_id, query_source, model and timestamp [CLAUDE-CODE], hold count, how many system blocks and tools the request holds, and hold each response member kept beside its content (messages.mtsv › kept.1) that is neither null nor empty.
values.5	Every request and its response MUST rebuild exactly from the values: the first messages of the request it extends, as many as kept, then its own; the system blocks and tools of the request it extends, as many as count gives, each replaced where it gives its own; its response from its own value. A member that is null, empty or absent gives the model nothing alike, and rebuilds as absent.
values.6	The module MUST read only the index lines of an input after the last index_line its output holds, a request's parent sought among the latest requests of each of the input's threads the output holds and those read since, each read again from its files.
values.7	Data about one type of observational unit is often split into files by another variable, the file's name its value [WICKHAM2014], and an event's timestamp is an ISO 8601 timestamp [CLAUDE-CODE]. One input is one session: the module MUST give the index lines of each session_id as an input apart, named by its date, the first ten characters of the timestamp of its first index line, then '/', then its session_id, the inputs in the order of their first index lines.
map.1	The verbs of data manipulation are filter, transform, aggregate and sort, each possibly by group [WICKHAM2014]. The module MUST give, for a set of an input's values, grouped by session_id and query_source, in the order of their first index lines: how many requests, and the first and last index_line and timestamp.
map.2	The requests of an input form a tree by extends (values.2). The module MUST give the lineage of an input's latest request: that request, then the request it extends, and so on until a request that extends none.
